      <main class="content">
        <div class="page-header">
          <span class="badge badge-advanced">Advanced &middot; 35 min</span>
          <h1>Tokens, JWT, Claims &amp; Endpoints</h1>
          <p class="lead">Deep dive into token architectures, JSON Web Token (JWT) verification, cryptographic signature verification (RS256 / JWKS), standard claims, introspection, revocation, and WAM policies.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- TOKEN CONSUMERS -->
        <section id="token-consumers">
          <h2>Token Types &amp; Intended Consumers</h2>
          <p>Every token in the OAuth / OIDC ecosystem is minted for a specific consumer. Routing a token to the wrong consumer creates severe security vulnerabilities:</p>

          <pre class="mermaid">
graph TD
    subgraph AuthServer["🔑 Authorization Server (IdP)"]
        TokenGen["Issue Token Pair"]
    end

    subgraph ClientApp["💻 Client Application (Relying Party)"]
        IDT["👤 Consumes ID Token<br/>(Reads user profile, sub, email)"]
        RT["🔄 Stores Refresh Token<br/>(HTTP-only cookie / Secure vault)"]
        AT_Send["📤 Forwards Access Token"]
    end

    subgraph APIGateway["🛡️ API Gateway / Resource Server"]
        JWKS["Verifies RS256 with JWKS"]
        RS["🗄️ Consumes Access Token<br/>(Evaluates scopes, roles &amp; RBAC)"]
    end

    TokenGen --&gt;|"ID Token (Signed JWT)"| IDT
    TokenGen --&gt;|"Refresh Token"| RT
    TokenGen --&gt;|"Access Token (Bearer)"| AT_Send

    AT_Send --&gt;|"Authorization: Bearer &lt;Access Token&gt;"| JWKS
    JWKS --&gt; RS
    RT -.-&gt;|"POST /token (grant_type=refresh_token)"| TokenGen
          </pre>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Token Type</th><th>Intended Consumer</th><th>Format</th><th>Security Invariant &amp; Lifetime</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Access Token</strong></td>
                <td>Resource Server (API)</td>
                <td>JWT or Opaque</td>
                <td>Short-lived (15–60 min). Presented in <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header. <strong>Never inspect inside client apps.</strong></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>ID Token</strong></td>
                <td>Client Application</td>
                <td>Signed JWT</td>
                <td>Short-lived (5–10 min). Proves user authentication. <strong>Never send to backend APIs as Bearer tokens!</strong></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Refresh Token</strong></td>
                <td>Authorization Server</td>
                <td>Opaque string</td>
                <td>Long-lived (days/weeks). Used exclusively to fetch fresh access tokens. Stored in HTTP-only cookies or secret vaults.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- JWT HISTORY -->
        <section id="jwt-history">
          <h2>The Evolution of Tokens: Opaque to JWE</h2>
          <p>How token standards evolved across the internet:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Opaque Random Strings (Pre-2012):</strong> Tokens were completely opaque random strings (e.g. <code>SlAV32hkKG</code>). Every API call required the Resource Server to make a synchronous back-channel database lookup or introspection call to the Authorization Server to check validity and user identity.</li>
            <li><strong>JSON Web Tokens (RFC 7519, 2015):</strong> Encoded self-contained identity and access claims into structured JSON. Allows APIs to validate expiration (<code>exp</code>) and scopes locally without database round-trips.</li>
            <li><strong>JSON Web Signature (JWS - RFC 7515):</strong> Added cryptographic signatures (RS256, ES256) to ensure the token payload cannot be forged or tampered with.</li>
            <li><strong>JSON Web Encryption (JWE - RFC 7516):</strong> Encrypts the payload for end-to-end confidentiality when passing sensitive PII across untrusted intermediaries.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- BEARER RISK -->
        <section id="bearer-risk">
          <h2>Bearer Token Masquerade Risk &amp; Mitigation</h2>
          <div class="callout danger">
            <strong>⚠️ The Bearer Token Security Invariant:</strong>
            <p>Access tokens are standard <em>Bearer</em> tokens: <strong>Whoever "bears" the token can access the authorized resource without secondary proof of identity.</strong> If an attacker steals your access token from browser storage or an insecure log file, they can immediately masquerade as you until the token expires.</p>
            <p><strong>Required Mitigations:</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Enforce short token lifetimes (15 to 60 minutes max).</li>
              <li>Always transmit over TLS 1.3 / HTTPS.</li>
              <li>Store tokens in <code>Secure</code>, <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=Strict</code> cookies rather than <code>localStorage</code>.</li>
              <li>Implement Mutual TLS (mTLS - RFC 8705) or DPoP (Demonstrating Proof of Possession - RFC 9449) for high-security zero trust environments.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SCOPES VS CLAIMS -->
        <section id="scopes-vs-claims">
          <h2>Scope vs Claim vs Response Type: The 3 Pillars</h2>
          <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap:1rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">1. Scope</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The Request (Authorization)</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Permissions requested by the Client during the authorization request (e.g. <code>read:reports</code>, <code>openid</code>).</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">2. Claim</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The Assertion (Identity &amp; Context)</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Key-value attribute packed inside a signed JWT payload (e.g. <code>"sub": "usr_123"</code>, <code>"roles": ["Admin"]</code>).</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">3. Response Type</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The Protocol Instruction</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Tells the IdP what token artifacts to return in the front-channel redirect (e.g. <code>code</code>, <code>id_token</code>).</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- JWT STRUCTURE -->
        <section id="jwt-structure">
          <h2>JWT Anatomy &amp; Tamper-Proofing (RFC 7519)</h2>
          <p>A JSON Web Token consists of three base64url-encoded strings separated by periods: <code>header.payload.signature</code></p>
          
          <div style="background:#070c14; border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.5rem; border-radius:var(--radius); margin:1.5rem 0; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.9rem;">
            <div style="color:#f87171; word-break:break-all; margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
              <span style="font-weight:700;">HEADER:</span> eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCIsImtpZCI6ImtleV8yMDI2XzAxIn0
            </div>
            <div style="color:#c084fc; word-break:break-all; margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
              <span style="font-weight:700;">PAYLOAD:</span> eyJpc3MiOiJodHRwczovL2F1dGhtYXRyaXgubG9jYWwiLCJzdWIiOiJ1c3JfYWxpY2VfMTIzIiwicm9sZXMiOlsiQWRtaW4iXX0
            </div>
            <div style="color:#38bdf8; word-break:break-all;">
              <span style="font-weight:700;">SIGNATURE:</span> Td4k7wN5rXyZ_8Pq9LmVoA...
            </div>
          </div>

          <pre><code><span class="c">// 1. HEADER (Specifies Algorithm and Key ID)</span>
&#123;
  "alg": "RS256",
  "typ": "JWT",
  "kid": "key_2026_01"
&#125;

<span class="c">// 2. PAYLOAD (Identity and Access Claims)</span>
&#123;
  "iss": "https://authmatrix.local",
  "sub": "usr_alice_123",
  "aud": "https://api.authmatrix.local",
  "exp": 1755003600,
  "iat": 1755000000,
  "roles": ["Admin"],
  "permissions": ["read:reports", "write:users", "delete:audit"]
&#125;

<span class="c">// 3. SIGNATURE (Asymmetric RSA SHA-256)</span>
RS256(Base64URL(Header) + "." + Base64URL(Payload), PrivateKey)</code></pre>
        </section>

        <!-- ACCESS TOKEN PAYLOAD -->
        <section id="access-token-payload">
          <h2>Access Token Payload Anatomy (Okta &amp; Enterprise Standard)</h2>
          <p>While OAuth 2.0 does not dictate access token formats, modern enterprise providers (Okta, Entra ID, Auth0) issue signed JWT access tokens packed with authorization metadata:</p>

          <pre><code><span class="c">// Decoded Enterprise Access Token (JWT Payload)</span>
&#123;
  "ver": 1,
  "jti": "AT.upPJqU-Ism6Fwt5Fpl8AhNAdoUeuMsEgJ_VxJ3WJ1hk",
  "iss": "https://authmatrix.local/oauth2/default",
  "aud": "https://api.authmatrix.local",
  "sub": "alice@authmatrix.local",
  "uid": "00u2yulup4eWbOttd1t7",
  "iat": 1755000000,
  "exp": 1755003600,
  "cid": "0oa2yrbf35Vcbom491t7",
  "scp": ["openid", "profile", "email", "read:reports", "write:users"],
  "roles": ["Admin", "SecurityEngineer"]
&#125;</code></pre>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Claim Key</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Security Function</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>scp</code></td><td>Array of approved scope strings</td><td>Evaluated by API Gateway / backend route middleware for Least Privilege RBAC.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>uid</code> / <code>sub</code></td><td>User Unique Identifier</td><td>Identifies user context for DB audits and row-level multi-tenancy.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>cid</code> / <code>client_id</code></td><td>Application Identifier</td><td>Identifies calling application (Client) for throttling and rate limiting.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>jti</code></td><td>JWT ID</td><td>Unique random token GUID; cached by APIs to detect and reject token replay.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- CLAIMS -->
        <section id="claims">
          <h2>Standard &amp; Custom JWT Claims</h2>
          
          <h3>1. Standard JWT Claims (RFC 7519 Section 4.1)</h3>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Claim</th><th>Name</th><th>Verification Rule</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>iss</code></td><td>Issuer</td><td>Must match the exact URL of the Authorization Server.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>sub</code></td><td>Subject</td><td>Unique permanent identifier of the user/entity.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>aud</code></td><td>Audience</td><td>Must match the API identifier / Client ID. Rejects misrouted tokens.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>exp</code></td><td>Expiration</td><td>Unix timestamp. Reject if <code>now() &gt; exp</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>nbf</code></td><td>Not Before</td><td>Unix timestamp. Reject if <code>now() &lt; nbf</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>iat</code></td><td>Issued At</td><td>Unix timestamp indicating token creation time.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>jti</code></td><td>JWT ID</td><td>Unique token GUID used to prevent replay attacks.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- SIGNING -->
        <section id="signing">
          <h2>RS256 vs HS256 Cryptography</h2>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>HS256 (Symmetric)</th><th>RS256 (Asymmetric)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Key Mechanism</strong></td><td>Single Shared Secret Key</td><td>Private Key (Signs) + Public Key (Verifies)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Key Distribution Risk</strong></td><td><strong>High Risk:</strong> Every API service must store the shared secret. If one API is breached, the attacker can mint tokens for all services.</td><td><strong>Zero Secret Leakage:</strong> Only the IdP holds the private key. APIs only download public keys.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Enterprise Standard</strong></td><td>Internal legacy services only</td><td><strong>Industry Gold Standard</strong> for all microservices &amp; cloud architectures.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- JWKS -->
        <section id="jwks">
          <h2>Dynamic JWKS Key Verification (RFC 7517)</h2>
          <p>APIs verify incoming tokens without calling the Authorization Server on every request. Instead, they dynamically download public keys from <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>:</p>

          <pre><code>GET /.well-known/jwks.json HTTP/1.1
Host: accounts.example.com

<span class="c">// Response</span>
&#123;
  "keys": [
    &#123;
      "kty": "RSA",
      "use": "sig",
      "alg": "RS256",
      "kid": "key_2026_01",
      "n": "u1WguZo5WOk5Y...",
      "e": "AQAB"
    &#125;
  ]
&#125;</code></pre>
          <p><strong>Key Rotation:</strong> When an IdP rotates signing keys, it publishes the new key in the JWKS array. The API matches the JWT header's <code>kid</code> (Key ID) to the JWKS key without downtime.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ENDPOINTS -->
        <section id="endpoints">
          <h2>The Four OAuth Endpoints</h2>

          <h3>1. Token Introspection (RFC 7662)</h3>
          <p>Allows Resource Servers to validate opaque tokens or check real-time revocation status:</p>
          <pre><code>POST /introspect HTTP/1.1
Host: authorization-server.example.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Authorization: Basic Y2xpZW50X2lkOmNsaWVudF9zZWNyZXQ=

token=2YotnFZFEjr1zCsicMWpAA

<span class="c">// Response</span>
&#123;
  "active": true,
  "scope": "read:reports write:users",
  "client_id": "client_app_123",
  "sub": "usr_alice_123",
  "exp": 1755003600
&#125;</code></pre>

          <h3>2. Token Revocation (RFC 7009)</h3>
          <p>Explicitly invalidates an active token upon user logout:</p>
          <pre><code>POST /revoke HTTP/1.1
Host: authorization-server.example.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

token=8xLOxBtZp8&amp;token_type_hint=refresh_token</code></pre>
        </section>

        <!-- WAM -->
        <section id="wam">
          <h2>Web Access Management (WAM) Policies</h2>
          <p>Modern Zero Trust policy engines evaluate token claims and environmental signals in real time before granting access to sensitive business operations:</p>

          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:0.75rem; font-size:1.05rem;">🛡️ WAM Real-Time Policy Evaluation Matrix</div>
            <ul style="margin-bottom:0; line-height:1.7;">
              <li><strong>User Identity Context:</strong> <code>sub</code>, <code>roles</code>, verified email domain.</li>
              <li><strong>Resource Context:</strong> Requested endpoint (e.g. <code>DELETE /api/protected/audit</code>) and required permission (<code>delete:audit</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>Environmental Risk Signals:</strong> IP Geolocation anomaly, corporate managed device status, and MFA assurance level (<code>acr</code> / <code>amr</code>).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <footer class="page-footer">
          <div class="prev-next">
            <a href={base + '/oidc'}>&larr; OIDC</a>
            <a href={base + '/api-guide'}>Next: API Guide &rarr;</a>
          </div>
        </footer>
      </main>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
